sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/agent/delphisci-resolvability-assessor-grok-4.7-high-fast> a prov:SoftwareAgent;
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "delphisci dataset-resolvability assessor (Grok 4.7)";
    prov:actedOnBehalfOf <https://orcid.org/0009-0007-4948-9192>;
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/usesModel> <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/model/grok-4.7-high-fast>.
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/model/grok-4.7-high-fast> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/LanguageModel>;
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Grok 4.7";
    dc:identifier "grok-4.7-high-fast".
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/thompson1917-ch09-03--grok> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/LLMClaimAssessment>;
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Grok 4.7 on thompson1917-ch09-03: partially-resolvable, resolvability 0.37";
    dc:description "Only the physical adequacy of a non-living polarizing force is measurable with existing photochemistry and atmospheric optics, and those results sit in papers rather than in one open dataset. Nothing public records the polarization and chemistry of the prebiotic sea surface at the origin of homochirality. The hypothesis is therefore only weakly separable from other asymmetric forces, and only the proxy, not the origin, can be examined now.";
    dc:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/concept/dataset-resolvability-0.2.0>;
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Proposed test with existing data: Test two proxies: whether sunlight reflected from the sea is sufficiently circularly polarized, and whether that polarization produces a measurable enantiomeric excess in relevant molecules. Either failure weakens the proposed starting force; success still leaves rival origins of biological homochirality standing.";
    prov:wasAttributedTo <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/agent/delphisci-resolvability-assessor-grok-4.7-high-fast>;
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/assessmentType> "dataset-resolvability/0.2.0";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/evidenceAgainst> "Parity violation, chance amplification and other chiral biases predict the same biological handedness.", "The wording is that asymmetry may have started this way, so a successful laboratory effect does not identify the historical cause.";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/hasConfidence> "0.5"^^xsd:double;
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/hasScore> "0.373"^^xsd:double;
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/hasVerdict> "partially-resolvable".
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/thompson1917-ch09-03--grok--analysis-feasibility> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/LLMClaimAssessment>;
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Analysis feasibility of thompson1917-ch09-03 by Grok 4.7: 0.35";
    dc:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/concept/dataset-resolvability-0.2.0-analysis-feasibility>;
    prov:wasAttributedTo <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/agent/delphisci-resolvability-assessor-grok-4.7-high-fast>;
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/assessmentType> "dataset-resolvability/0.2.0/analysis-feasibility";
